1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,925
Welcome to Session twelve, the last lecture of the course.

2
00:00:03,925 --> 00:00:11,025
Session six treated protein structure as something you look up: here is a structure, here is what you can measure on it.

3
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:13,700
Today we ask the other question.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,225
Given only the sequence, can we predict the structure?

5
00:00:17,225 --> 00:00:23,900
And then, having answered that, can we go further and design sequences that fold into structures we choose?

6
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:28,375
This is the session where everything in the course arrives in one place.

7
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:31,250
Session four and five's alignments.

8
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:33,925
Session seven's hidden layers.

9
00:00:33,925 --> 00:00:36,575
Session nine's backpropagation.

10
00:00:36,575 --> 00:00:41,175
Session ten's residual convolutional networks on contact maps.

11
00:00:41,175 --> 00:00:43,625
Session eleven's attention.

12
00:00:43,625 --> 00:00:49,050
All of them are components of AlphaFold2, and I will point at each as it appears.

13
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:50,675
Three blocks.

14
00:00:50,675 --> 00:00:57,850
First, where long-range structural information comes from, which is the question the whole field was stuck on for fifty years.

15
00:00:57,850 --> 00:01:05,600
Second, how AlphaFold2 combines the course's ideas, and, just as importantly, how much you should trust what it gives you.

16
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:11,525
Third, complexes, generative models, and protein design, which is where the field is now.

17
00:01:11,525 --> 00:01:20,425
And the thread running through all three, which I would like you to carry out of this course: a model is a hypothesis with a confidence estimate attached.

18
00:01:20,425 --> 00:01:22,825
It is not an experiment.

19
00:01:22,825 --> 00:01:28,900
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session twelve page of the course book.

20
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:37,125
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

21
00:01:37,125 --> 00:01:41,150
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

22
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:46,350
Look at this slide, which lists what each session contributes to today.

23
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:50,600
And then the question written on it, which is the question of Block one.

24
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:56,975
If we try to predict a residue's structure from its local sequence alone, what information is missing?

25
00:01:56,975 --> 00:02:04,225
Think back to Session seven, where we had a ten-residue stretch that was a helix in one protein and a strand in another.

26
00:02:04,225 --> 00:02:07,725
Identical local sequence, opposite answers.

27
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:12,425
What is missing is the contacts with residues far away in the sequence.

28
00:02:12,425 --> 00:02:20,925
Whether this stretch forms a helix depends on what it is packed against, and what it is packed against may be two hundred residues away along the chain.

29
00:02:20,925 --> 00:02:29,250
That is the whole problem of structure prediction in one sentence, and Block one is the fifty-year story of trying to get at that information.

30
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:31,325
Block one.

31
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:34,900
Where does long-range structural information come from?

32
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:40,525
On the page, this block is the section "Where does long-range structural information come from?".

33
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:55,975
We start from the biophysics, so that everyone is on the same footing, then the two classic observations that define the problem, then what people actually did before deep learning, and then the idea that broke it open: that residues in contact evolve together.

34
00:02:56,575 --> 00:03:02,450
First, what folding is, briefly, because Session six covered structure rather than folding.

35
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:06,075
A protein is made as a line of amino acids.

36
00:03:06,075 --> 00:03:13,350
In water, it folds into a specific three-dimensional shape, and it does so for reasons that are mostly understandable.

37
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:16,750
The main driver is the hydrophobic effect.

38
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:25,325
Some side chains are greasy and do not mix with water, so the chain arranges itself to bury those on the inside, away from the solvent.

39
00:03:25,325 --> 00:03:28,650
That single tendency does most of the work.

40
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:37,500
Then the backbone forms hydrogen bonds with itself, and the regular ways of doing that are the helices and strands of Session seven's secondary structure.

41
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:46,100
And then the core packs tightly, which constrains which residues can sit next to which, since a large side chain cannot occupy a small space.

42
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:56,725
The conventional four levels: primary structure is the sequence, secondary is helices and strands, tertiary is the fold of one chain, quaternary is several chains together.

43
00:03:56,725 --> 00:04:03,700
One thing worth knowing about the result: the folded state is only slightly more stable than the unfolded chain.

44
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:10,200
The whole structure hangs on a small energy difference, which is why a single mutation can matter so much.

45
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:23,225
The classic example is in our own running protein: in beta-globin, glutamate six changed to valine puts a hydrophobic residue on the surface, the molecules stick to each other, and that is sickle-cell disease.

46
00:04:23,225 --> 00:04:27,175
And finally, the economics that make prediction worth doing.

47
00:04:27,175 --> 00:04:31,375
UniProt holds about a hundred and fifty million sequences.

48
00:04:31,375 --> 00:04:35,700
The PDB holds about two hundred and sixty thousand structures.

49
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,450
Fewer than one in five hundred.

50
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:47,400
Sequencing is cheap and fast; solving a structure by X-ray crystallography, NMR or cryo-electron microscopy takes months to years.

51
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:50,250
Prediction is how you fill that gap.

52
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:57,450
Two results from the nineteen sixties and seventies define the problem, and they pull in opposite directions.

53
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,950
The first is Anfinsen's.

54
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:07,125
He took ribonuclease, unfolded it completely with urea and a reducing agent, and then removed those.

55
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:16,075
It refolded, by itself, into its working structure, forming the correct four disulphide bonds out of a hundred and five possible pairings.

56
00:05:16,075 --> 00:05:17,525
No cell.

57
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:19,250
No template.

58
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:21,050
No machinery.

59
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:24,150
Just the sequence and the right conditions.

60
00:05:24,150 --> 00:05:30,125
That is the result that makes this field possible, because it says the information is in the sequence.

61
00:05:30,125 --> 00:05:36,725
If the sequence determines the structure, then predicting the structure from the sequence is possible in principle.

62
00:05:36,725 --> 00:05:40,950
Anfinsen received the Nobel Prize in nineteen seventy-two.

63
00:05:40,950 --> 00:05:45,725
With caveats, which matter in practice: not every protein does this.

64
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:47,850
Some need chaperones.

65
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:50,975
Some only fold when bound to a partner.

66
00:05:50,975 --> 00:05:53,425
Some switch between states.

67
00:05:53,425 --> 00:05:57,750
And some regions never fold at all, which we will come back to.

68
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:02,275
The second observation is Levinthal's, and it pulls the other way.

69
00:06:02,275 --> 00:06:08,100
Take a protein of a hundred residues, and allow just three possible states per residue.

70
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:13,800
That is three to the hundred, about five times ten to the forty-seven conformations.

71
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:21,000
If you could try ten to the thirteen per second, you would need five times ten to the thirty-four seconds to try them all.

72
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,525
The universe is about four times ten to the seventeen seconds old.

73
00:06:25,525 --> 00:06:29,600
And yet real proteins fold in microseconds to seconds.

74
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,675
So folding cannot be a random search.

75
00:06:32,675 --> 00:06:44,300
The resolution is the folding funnel: local structure forms quickly, and every step that buries hydrophobic groups lowers the free energy, so the chain is guided downhill rather than wandering.

76
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:54,750
And the lesson for us, which is why I show it in a computational course: every method, whether it simulates physics or not, needs a smarter search than trying everything.

77
00:06:54,750 --> 00:06:58,750
There is not enough time in the universe for the brute-force version.

78
00:06:59,350 --> 00:07:06,250
Four approaches dominated before deep learning, and each is still worth knowing because each captures something real.

79
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:09,325
Homology modelling: copy a relative.

80
00:07:09,325 --> 00:07:19,950
Find a protein of known structure that is homologous to yours, align the sequences using Sessions four and five, and copy the coordinates across, adjusting where they differ.

81
00:07:19,950 --> 00:07:27,750
This works because, as Chothia and Lesk showed in nineteen eighty-six, structure is conserved far more strongly than sequence.

82
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:31,600
It works well above about thirty per cent identity.

83
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:38,075
And its quality is limited by your alignment: a model is only as good as the alignment it was built from.

84
00:07:38,075 --> 00:07:41,275
With no template, you have nothing to copy.

85
00:07:41,275 --> 00:07:44,000
Threading: find a fold that fits.

86
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:56,575
Instead of searching for a similar sequence, take every known fold and ask how well your sequence could sit on it, scoring whether hydrophobic residues end up buried and whether contacting residues are compatible.

87
00:07:56,575 --> 00:08:00,000
This finds templates that sequence search misses.

88
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,200
It later gave way largely to profile-profile searching, which is Session five.

89
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,175
Fragment assembly, which is Rosetta.

90
00:08:08,175 --> 00:08:14,550
Local sequence biases local structure, which is Session seven's secondary-structure prediction.

91
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:25,600
So take three-residue and nine-residue pieces of known structures that match your local sequence, and assemble them by Monte Carlo search scored with a knowledge-based energy function.

92
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:29,550
Make thousands of candidate structures, keep the best-scoring.

93
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:34,600
This could produce genuinely new folds, but mostly for small proteins.

94
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:41,000
It grew into protein design, and David Baker shared the twenty twenty-four Nobel Prize for that.

95
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,350
Molecular dynamics: simulate the physics.

96
00:08:44,350 --> 00:08:48,150
Newton's laws, with time steps of two femtoseconds.

97
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:56,800
It is the most principled approach and the slowest by a very long way: folding needs something like ten to the nine to ten to the fifteen steps.

98
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:05,400
In twenty eleven, the special-purpose Anton machine folded twelve small proteins, which was a landmark and is still nowhere near routine.

99
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:07,675
And what did all four share?

100
00:09:07,675 --> 00:09:12,450
They all struggled with the same thing: getting the long-range contacts right.

101
00:09:13,050 --> 00:09:17,125
Let me make that explicit, because it is the hinge of the block.

102
00:09:17,125 --> 00:09:23,400
Session seven showed you ten residues that were a helix in one protein and a strand in another.

103
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:27,700
Identical sequence; we called them chameleon sequences.

104
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:31,975
No method that looks only at a local window can get both right.

105
00:09:31,975 --> 00:09:41,700
That is not a limitation of the method, it is a property of the problem: the same input genuinely has two different answers, and what distinguishes them is elsewhere.

106
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:44,225
Elsewhere means the contacts.

107
00:09:44,225 --> 00:09:51,625
What this stretch is packed against decides what it becomes, and those partners can be hundreds of residues away along the chain.

108
00:09:51,625 --> 00:10:00,325
So the question for the rest of this block is: where could information about those long-range contacts possibly come from, given only the sequence?

109
00:10:00,325 --> 00:10:05,175
And the answer, which took the field twenty years to make work, is evolution.

110
00:10:05,775 --> 00:10:11,675
This slide is the shape of the field over fifty years, and it is worth seeing before the details.

111
00:10:11,675 --> 00:10:17,225
The first generation is physics and templates: the four approaches on the previous slide.

112
00:10:17,225 --> 00:10:23,175
Steady, incremental progress for proteins that had a template, and very little for the rest.

113
00:10:23,175 --> 00:10:28,175
If your protein had no relative with a known structure, you were largely stuck.

114
00:10:28,175 --> 00:10:36,100
The second generation is coevolution plus deep learning, which is the rest of this block and which arrived around twenty sixteen.

115
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:40,500
The third is AlphaFold2 and what followed, which is Block two.

116
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:50,200
And the thing that distinguishes them is exactly what we have been discussing: the first generation had no source of long-range information, and the second found one.

117
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:53,850
Here is the idea, and it is beautiful.

118
00:10:53,850 --> 00:10:57,850
Take two residues that touch each other in the folded structure.

119
00:10:57,850 --> 00:11:01,275
Suppose one of them mutates to something larger.

120
00:11:01,275 --> 00:11:07,400
If the protein is to keep working, the partner it touches often has to become smaller to make room.

121
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:14,600
Or a positive charge that loses its negative partner is bad, so when one changes the other tends to change with it.

122
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:22,550
Now look not at one protein but at a large family of homologs, a multiple sequence alignment with thousands of sequences.

123
00:11:22,550 --> 00:11:28,350
Evolution has been running this experiment for us, independently, in every lineage.

124
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:37,075
Positions that are in contact will show correlated changes down the alignment, while positions that never touch will vary independently.

125
00:11:37,075 --> 00:11:41,675
So: find the correlated pairs of columns, and you have found the contacts.

126
00:11:41,675 --> 00:11:47,975
And contacts are exactly the long-range information that everything in the previous slides was missing.

127
00:11:47,975 --> 00:11:57,450
This was noticed in nineteen ninety-four, by two groups around Chris Sander at EMBL: one with Göbel and Valencia, one with Shindyalov and Kolchanov.

128
00:11:57,450 --> 00:12:03,425
And they found a real signal: the most correlated pairs were in contact more often than chance.

129
00:12:03,425 --> 00:12:05,575
And then it did not work.

130
00:12:05,575 --> 00:12:11,200
Most predicted pairs were wrong, and the signal was far too weak to fold a protein with.

131
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:15,575
The idea sat there, correct and useless, for about two decades.

132
00:12:15,575 --> 00:12:18,525
The next slide is the main reason why.

133
00:12:19,125 --> 00:12:24,225
Simple correlation has two problems, and the second one is the serious one.

134
00:12:24,225 --> 00:12:26,575
The first is phylogeny.

135
00:12:26,575 --> 00:12:32,650
Sequences in an alignment are related to each other by descent, not drawn independently.

136
00:12:32,650 --> 00:12:43,525
Two positions can appear correlated simply because a group of closely related sequences happens to share both residues, inherited together, with no structural meaning at all.

137
00:12:43,525 --> 00:12:48,650
The second is indirect coupling, and it is the one that really spoils things.

138
00:12:48,650 --> 00:12:53,450
Suppose residue A touches residue B, and residue B touches residue C.

139
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:56,600
A and B are coupled, B and C are coupled.

140
00:12:56,600 --> 00:13:03,275
Now look at A and C: they will appear correlated too, through B, even though A and C never touch each other.

141
00:13:03,275 --> 00:13:13,425
And in a protein, where every residue in the core touches several others, these indirect chains are everywhere, which is why the correlated pairs were mostly not contacts.

142
00:13:13,425 --> 00:13:18,025
The fix is direct coupling analysis, and the key word is global.

143
00:13:18,025 --> 00:13:30,325
Instead of looking at each pair of columns on its own, fit a single model to the whole alignment at once: a field for each residue at each position, and a coupling for each pair of residues at each pair of positions.

144
00:13:30,325 --> 00:13:40,925
Because the parameters are fitted together, the A-to-B and B-to-C couplings already account for the apparent A-to-C correlation, so the fitted A-to-C coupling stays near zero.

145
00:13:40,925 --> 00:13:45,500
It separates the direct from the indirect, which is what the name says.

146
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:56,350
On CheY, the protein we will look at next, precision rises from nought point four nine with simple mutual information to nought point seven three with direct coupling analysis.

147
00:13:56,350 --> 00:13:58,075
Two footnotes.

148
00:13:58,075 --> 00:14:07,475
Technically this fits the maximum-entropy model that reproduces the single-column and column-pair frequencies of the alignment, which is Session one's material.

149
00:14:07,475 --> 00:14:16,625
Physicists call the same object a Potts model, a generalisation of the Ising model to more than two states, and you will meet that name in the literature.

150
00:14:16,625 --> 00:14:32,400
Fitting it exactly is out of reach, so implementations approximate: mean-field methods invert a covariance matrix, while p-l-m-D-C-A and GREMLIN maximise a pseudo-likelihood instead, which is slower and better, and is what most later work uses.

151
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:56,300
And a nice piece of history: Lapedes and colleagues at Los Alamos identified both problems, phylogeny and indirect couplings, in nineteen ninety-nine, in a statistics lecture-note series that almost no biologist read, and fitted this model to protein alignments in a two thousand and two manuscript that was only posted to arXiv in twenty twelve, after direct coupling analysis had made the idea famous.

152
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:59,725
Being right early is not the same as being heard.

153
00:14:59,725 --> 00:15:10,875
And one connection worth carrying away: because these couplings capture how positions depend on each other, the same model also predicts mutation effects better than an independent profile does.

154
00:15:10,875 --> 00:15:21,650
That is EVmutation, and it is exactly the epistasis we measured in GB1 in Session nine, learned from an alignment instead of from twenty thousand measured variants.

155
00:15:22,250 --> 00:15:27,325
Here are all of those methods on one protein, which makes the progress concrete.

156
00:15:27,325 --> 00:15:32,825
CheY, a hundred and twenty-eight residues, PDB entry three C H Y.

157
00:15:32,825 --> 00:15:42,600
An alignment of eleven thousand and eighty-four sequences, with an effective number of about eight thousand nine hundred and twenty after accounting for redundancy.

158
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:51,025
We take each method's top hundred and twenty-eight predicted contacts and plot them on the real contact map, blue for correct and red for wrong.

159
00:15:51,025 --> 00:15:56,550
Mutual information, which is plain correlation: precision nought point four nine.

160
00:15:56,550 --> 00:15:59,125
Half of its predictions are wrong.

161
00:15:59,125 --> 00:16:07,750
And look at where the red is: the false positives cluster in blocks, which is the signature of phylogeny and conservation rather than structure.

162
00:16:07,750 --> 00:16:11,500
Direct coupling analysis: nought point seven three.

163
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:19,950
The red that remains is scattered rather than clustered, which tells you the systematic artefact has been removed and what is left is noise.

164
00:16:19,950 --> 00:16:25,025
Deep learning, here a contact head from ESM-2: nought point eight nine.

165
00:16:25,025 --> 00:16:28,950
And an AlphaFold2 model: nought point nine nine.

166
00:16:28,950 --> 00:16:35,125
Nought point four nine to nought point nine nine, over about twenty-five years, on the same alignment.

167
00:16:35,125 --> 00:16:38,725
The information was in that alignment the whole time.

168
00:16:38,725 --> 00:16:42,200
What improved was our ability to extract it.

169
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:56,450
One note on the method: the ESM-2 contact head stands in for the dedicated contact predictors of twenty seventeen to twenty nineteen, whose software no longer installs, which is itself a small lesson about research software.

170
00:16:57,050 --> 00:17:02,850
Now the step that turns a good contact predictor into a very good one, and it is Session ten.

171
00:17:02,850 --> 00:17:06,475
A contact map is not a random scatter of dots.

172
00:17:06,475 --> 00:17:10,625
Contacts come in patterns, because structure comes in patterns.

173
00:17:10,625 --> 00:17:14,000
A helix gives a thick band along the diagonal.

174
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:22,100
A pair of beta strands gives a line, running parallel to the diagonal if they are parallel, and perpendicular if they are antiparallel.

175
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:28,375
So if you know that one pair of residues is in contact, you know something about its neighbours in the map.

176
00:17:28,375 --> 00:17:30,575
Here is that measured.

177
00:17:30,575 --> 00:17:35,125
Take three-by-three windows around real contacts in eight structures.

178
00:17:35,125 --> 00:17:42,725
Of the two hundred and fifty-six possible on-off patterns such a window could show, only a hundred and forty-six ever occur.

179
00:17:42,725 --> 00:17:52,425
And if the centre pair is in contact, a neighbouring pair is in contact between twenty-three and forty-two per cent of the time, against a base rate of three per cent.

180
00:17:52,425 --> 00:18:07,250
So the map has strong local structure, which means a network that looks at a neighbourhood of the map can clean up a noisy coevolution signal: filling in the gaps of a half-detected strand pairing, removing isolated dots that no real fold would produce.

181
00:18:07,250 --> 00:18:18,050
And a network that looks at a local neighbourhood of a two-dimensional grid is a convolution, Session ten, exactly as that session promised when it said a contact map is an image.

182
00:18:18,050 --> 00:18:25,250
This is also, in essence, what a learned pair representation does inside AlphaFold2, which is Block two.

183
00:18:25,850 --> 00:18:32,900
One refinement before AlphaFold2, and it is a good example of making a problem easier by asking for more.

184
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:48,475
Instead of predicting a yes-or-no contact, predict a distance distribution for every pair of residues: not "these two touch" but "the distance between these two is probably between eight and ten ångström, possibly twelve, almost certainly not thirty".

185
00:18:48,475 --> 00:18:51,450
That is more informative in two ways.

186
00:18:51,450 --> 00:18:58,525
It carries a confidence, since a sharp distribution means the model is sure and a broad one means it is not.

187
00:18:58,525 --> 00:19:05,900
And it distinguishes close from very close, which a binary contact at an eight-ångström threshold throws away.

188
00:19:05,900 --> 00:19:14,625
Then you fold the protein by finding the coordinates that best satisfy all those distributions at once, which is a numerical optimisation.

189
00:19:14,625 --> 00:19:27,975
AlphaFold, the first version, and trRosetta, between twenty eighteen and twenty twenty, both did this with deep residual convolutional networks, which is Session ten's architecture doing real structural biology.

190
00:19:27,975 --> 00:19:36,300
trRosetta also predicted orientations between residues, not just distances, which constrains the geometry further.

191
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:41,050
And here is the whole of Block one, as one series of numbers.

192
00:19:41,050 --> 00:19:51,450
CASP, which you met in Session six, is the blind test: structures that have been solved but not published, predictions collected, scored when the experiments come out.

193
00:19:51,450 --> 00:19:59,300
The number here is the precision of the best group's long-range contact predictions on the hardest targets, the ones with no template.

194
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:04,350
Twenty ten to twenty fourteen: between twenty and twenty-seven per cent.

195
00:20:04,350 --> 00:20:07,150
Essentially flat for five years.

196
00:20:07,150 --> 00:20:10,350
Twenty sixteen: forty-seven per cent.

197
00:20:10,350 --> 00:20:13,775
That is deep learning on coevolution arriving.

198
00:20:13,775 --> 00:20:16,900
Twenty eighteen: above seventy per cent.

199
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:27,900
Twenty twenty: contacts are no longer even reported as the interesting quantity, because AlphaFold2 predicts structures directly and contacts fall out as a by-product.

200
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:30,500
Look at the shape of that series.

201
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:34,475
Flat, flat, flat, and then a step change in four years.

202
00:20:34,475 --> 00:20:41,075
This is one of the few places in biology where you can point at a date and say the problem changed character.

